                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 2,
                    "id": "obj-15",
                    "linecount": 2,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 8.0, 617.0, 429.95247524752483, 33.0 ],
                    "text": "If we further subdivide quarter tones, we get the half steps of half steps of half steps — or what we simply call \"eighth tones\" (6000, 6025, 6050, etc...)",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.819607843137255, 1.0, 0.905882352941176, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 2,
                    "id": "obj-14",
                    "linecount": 2,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 141.0, 581.5, 216.0, 33.0 ],
                    "text": "We give bach.ezmidiplay an argument of 4 so it plays back quarter tones. =>",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.819607843137255, 1.0, 0.905882352941176, 1.0 ],
                    "textjustification": 2
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 2,
                    "id": "obj-13",
                    "linecount": 7,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 8.0, 368.0, 384.95247524752483, 100.0 ],
                    "text": "In bach, we use MIDICENT values (MIDI note number * 100). So 60 becomes 6000, and this gives us 100 \"cents\" between each half step to express smaller degredations of pitch \"between the keys of the piano.\" We call these kinds of smaller tones \"microtones.\"\n\nFor example, if we divide all half steps on the piano in half, we obtain \"quarter tones\" — the half steps between half steps: 6000, 6050, etc...",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.819607843137255, 1.0, 0.905882352941176, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "fontface": 2,
                    "id": "obj-12",
                    "linecount": 3,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 8.0, 199.0, 429.95247524752483, 47.0 ],
                    "text": "In normal MIDI note numbering, adjacent integers are given for each half step between the notes of the keyboard. For example, below are all the notes between middle C and the next C above it; MIDI notes 60, 61, 62, ... 70.",
                    "textcolor": [ 0.819607843137255, 1.0, 0.905882352941176, 1.0 ]
                }
            },
            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-10",
                    "maxclass": "newobj",
                    "numinlets": 3,
                    "numoutlets": 2,
                    "outlettype": [ "", "int" ],
                    "patching_rect": [ 399.0, 373.0, 95.0, 22.0 ],
                    "text": "bach.ezmidiplay"
                }
            },
